<main>

<!-- ============ WHY ============ -->
<section class="panel active" id="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="sec-eyebrow">Why we built Rural Health Hub</div>
      <h2>Rural patients have higher mortality &mdash; because we never solved the logistics.</h2>
      <p class="intro">The gap isn't a medicine gap. Rural patients get the same drugs and the same guidelines as everyone else. What they don't get is access &mdash; and access is a logistics problem, which makes it a solvable one.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="whystat reveal">
      <div class="top">
        <div class="figure">
          <p class="fnum">20%</p>
          <p class="fcap">higher age-adjusted mortality for rural Americans than for urban Americans &mdash; a gap that has more than doubled since 1999.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="chartside">
          <h3>Age-adjusted mortality per 100,000</h3>
          <p class="cs">United States, 1999 compared with 2019</p>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 470 190" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: in 1999 urban mortality was 865 and rural 924 per 100,000, a 7 percent gap. By 2019 urban had fallen to 693 while rural fell only to 834, a 20 percent gap.">
            <g font-family="Hanken Grotesk,sans-serif">
              <text x="0" y="14" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#6E6557">1999</text>
              <rect x="0" y="24" width="288" height="20" rx="4" fill="#CFDCCF"/>
              <rect x="0" y="48" width="308" height="20" rx="4" fill="#369739"/>
              <text x="296" y="39" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" fill="#6E6557">865 urban</text>
              <text x="316" y="63" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" fill="#256827">924 rural</text>
              <text x="392" y="52" font-size="12" font-weight="800" fill="#6E6557">+7%</text>

              <text x="0" y="104" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#6E6557">2019</text>
              <rect x="0" y="114" width="231" height="20" rx="4" fill="#CFDCCF"/>
              <rect x="0" y="138" width="278" height="20" rx="4" fill="#F7960F"/>
              <text x="239" y="129" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" fill="#6E6557">693 urban</text>
              <text x="286" y="153" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" fill="#BC7009">834 rural</text>
              <text x="392" y="142" font-size="14" font-weight="800" fill="#BC7009">+20%</text>

              <text x="0" y="182" font-size="11" fill="#6E6557">Urban mortality fell faster. Rural progress stalled &mdash; the gap widened.</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="foot">
        <b>Not because the medicine is different. Because no one solved the distance.</b>
        <p>Specialist shortages and long drives mean rural patients defer care, skip follow-up, and arrive later and sicker. Every one of those is a coordination failure &mdash; not a clinical one.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="srcline"><b>Source:</b> CDC / National Center for Health Statistics, <i>Trends in Death Rates in Urban and Rural Areas: United States, 1999&ndash;2019</i>, NCHS Data Brief No. 417 (September 2021). Rates are age-adjusted per 100,000 population.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal" style="margin-top:46px">
      <div class="sec-eyebrow">How the gap actually happens</div>
      <h2>Three steps from distance to outcome.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="chain reveal">
      <div><div class="cn">Step one</div><h4>The specialist is far away</h4><p>A referral means a half-day of driving, time off work, fuel, and often a family member to drive. For many patients that cost is simply too high.</p></div>
      <div><div class="cn">Step two</div><h4>Care gets deferred</h4><p>The appointment is postponed, then missed, then never rescheduled. Follow-up after a hospitalization is the most commonly skipped visit of all.</p></div>
      <div><div class="cn">Step three</div><h4>The patient arrives later, and sicker</h4><p>What could have been managed in a clinic becomes an emergency, a transfer, or an admission &mdash; at the point where outcomes are worst and costs are highest.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal" style="margin-top:46px">
      <div class="sec-eyebrow">What we built it to produce</div>
      <h2>Four outcomes, one network.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="outc reveal">
      <div class="outc-row"><div class="on">01</div><div><b>Unparalleled access for patients</b><p>Specialty care close to home. Less travel, less time off work, fewer missed appointments &mdash; with referrals and follow-up coordinated on their behalf rather than left to them.</p></div></div>
      <div class="outc-row"><div class="on">02</div><div><b>Sustainability for rural hospitals</b><p>Increased utilization of the lab, imaging, procedures, and 340B pharmacy you already own, plus RHC-billable encounters that offset the cost of the program.</p></div></div>
      <div class="outc-row"><div class="on">03</div><div><b>Coordinated referrals and discharges for regional hospitals</b><p>Patients arrive already worked up and under active management, and return to a local clinic for follow-up &mdash; which is where readmissions get prevented.</p></div></div>
      <div class="outc-row"><div class="on">04</div><div><b>Appropriate utilization of healthcare resources</b><p>Care delivered at the right level, in the right place, at the right time &mdash; instead of an emergency department absorbing what a specialist could have answered in a ten-minute call.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

